Addendum: Deep-Dive into Matrix Construction — With Hand-Traceable Examples
This expands Sections 2 and 3 of the existing dry-run. Every claim below is followed by a toy numeric example small enough to verify with pencil and paper.

Toy model used throughout (unless stated otherwise):

State width S = 4 bits

Beat width W = 8 bits (one byte per beat)

Reflected polynomial POLY = 0b1100 (0xC, top bit = 1 so reverse step works)

Step function:

text
fb  = crc[0] XOR bit
crc = crc >> 1
if fb: crc ^= POLY
A. Why two matrices, not one
The state update for a full beat is:

text
new_state = (state-only contribution)  XOR  (data-only contribution)
Two independent input spaces:

Input	Width	Output	Matrix dim
old_state	32 bits	32-bit next state	A is 32×32
data	1024 bits	32-bit next state	B is 32×1024
They cannot be combined into one matrix because they act on different spaces and are injected at different "times" inside the beat (state bits feed the feedback on every step; data bits enter one at a time as each step's bit argument).

Toy dimensions (S=4, W=8): A is 4×4, B is 4×8. Different shapes — confirms they are distinct objects, not the same matrix reused.

B. Building A — one state bit at a time
B.1 The recipe
text
for i in range(32):
    s = 1 << i
    for k in range(W):
        s = step(s, 0)
    A_cols[i] = s
Each column answers the question: "If ONLY bit i of the old state is set, what does that single bit contribute to the next state after a full beat?"

Because the step is linear (Section 1 of the original doc), XOR-ing columns for the set bits of old_state reconstructs the answer for any old_state.

