C. Building B — one data bit at a time
C.1 The recipe
text
for j in range(1024):
    s = 0
    for k in range(1024):
        s = step(s, 1 if k == j else 0)
    B_cols[j] = s
Each column answers: "If ONLY data bit j is set in the beat, what does that single bit contribute to the next state?"

C.2 Key insight (why the trace simplifies)
Starting from s = 0:

Steps 0..j-1: bit = 0, so fb = 0 and the state stays at 0.

Step j: bit = 1, so fb = 1 and the state becomes POLY.

Steps j+1..W-1: bit = 0, so the state is just POLY advanced W-1-j more zero steps.

Define Z_k = POLY after k zero-data steps. Then:

text
B_cols[j] = Z_{W-1-j}
For the toy W = 8, POLY = 0xC:

k	Z_k
0	0xC
1	0x6
2	0x3
3	0xD
4	0xA
5	0x5
6	0xE
7	0x7
C.3 B in full
j	W-1-j	B_cols[j]
0	7	0x7
1	6	0xE
2	5	0x5
3	4	0xA
4	3	0xD
5	2	0x3
6	1	0x6
7	0	0xC
text
B_cols = [0x7, 0xE, 0x5, 0xA, 0xD, 0x3, 0x6, 0xC]
C.4 A curious identity
Look at the first four columns of B:

text
B_cols[0..3] = [0x7, 0xE, 0x5, 0xA]  ==  A_cols
This is not a coincidence. General derivation:

A_cols[i]: start with state e_i, feed W zeros. First i steps shift e_i down to e_0 (fb = 0 for all), then step i+1 sees state = e_0, so fb = 1 and state becomes POLY. Then W-1-i more zeros. Result: POLY through W-1-i zeros.

B_cols[i]: start with state 0, feed zeros for i steps (state stays 0), then one 1 bit at step i (fb = 1, state becomes POLY), then W-1-i more zeros. Result: POLY through W-1-i zeros.

They are the same quantity. In the real design, A has 32 columns and B has 1024 columns, but the lowest 32 columns of B equal A. This is exactly why, in the packed hex constants of the real RTL, the low 32 bits of B_GROUP0_MASK_PACKED[o] match A_MASK_PACKED[o] — you can verify this directly from the user-supplied constants (for example, row 31 of A_MASK_PACKED is 0xab8ed9b4, and the last 8 hex digits of row 31 of B_GROUP0_MASK_PACKED are ab8ed9b4).

D. From columns to rows (why the RTL uses row format)
The Python generators build column format — A_cols[i] is the output vector when input bit i alone is 1. This is natural for the probing procedure.

The RTL wants the row format — a mask per output bit that says "which input bits XOR into me."

Conversion: transpose the matrix.

For A in column form A_cols = [0x7, 0xE, 0x5, 0xA], decode each column to bits:

text
col 0 = 0x7 = 0b0111 → bits 0,1,2 set
col 1 = 0xE = 0b1110 → bits 1,2,3 set
col 2 = 0x5 = 0b0101 → bits 0,2 set
col 3 = 0xA = 0b1010 → bits 1,3 set
Transpose: for each output bit o, ask "which input bits set bit o in some column?"

output 0 appears in col 0 and col 2 → mask 0b0101 = 0x5

output 1 appears in col 0, col 1, col 3 → mask 0b1011 = 0xB

output 2 appears in col 0, col 1, col 2 → mask 0b0111 = 0x7

output 3 appears in col 1, col 3 → mask 0b1010 = 0xA

So row masks:

text
A_MASK_rows = [0x5, 0xB, 0x7, 0xA]
Verify with a specific state, state = 0b0011 (bits 0 and 1 set):

Column form: A_cols[0] XOR A_cols[1] = 0x7 XOR 0xE = 0x9.

Row form: output bit o = parity(state & A_MASK_rows[o]):

o=0: state & 0x5 = 0b0011 & 0b0101 = 0b0001 → parity = 1

o=1: state & 0xB = 0b0011 & 0b1011 = 0b0011 → parity = 0

o=2: state & 0x7 = 0b0011 & 0b0111 = 0b0011 → parity = 0

o=3: state & 0xA = 0b0011 & 0b1010 = 0b0010 → parity = 1

Result: 0b1001 = 0x9 ✓.

In the real RTL, A_MASK_PACKED[o*32 +: 32] is exactly this row-mask for output bit o, and the RTL computes y[o] = ^(state & A_MASK_PACKED[o*32 +: 32]) — the AND-XOR-reduce form.

E. The AND-XOR-reduce as gates
E.1 The collapse
For output bit o, the row mask tells you which inputs XOR into it. If the mask has, say, k ones, you need one k-input XOR (realized as XOR tree of k-1 2-input XORs).

E.2 Concrete gate example
Take toy A_MASK_rows[0] = 0x5 = 0b0101 — bits 0 and 2 set. Then:

text
y[0] = state[0] XOR state[2]
Two-input XOR gate. Even though the state is 4 bits wide, this output bit doesn't need any logic for bits 1 and 3 — they don't affect output bit 0.

E.3 Fan-in distribution (real design)
For the real 32-bit CRC with 1024-bit beat, the row masks in MASK_PACKED have average fan-in about 524 of the 1056 possible input bits (32 state + 1024 data). The min and max are roughly 501 and 554. This number is the reason the pipeline exists — 524-input XOR trees in one combinational shot would not close timing.

F. Constructing a mask constant — end to end
Suppose (toy) B_cols = [0x7, 0xE, 0x5, 0xA, 0xD, 0x3, 0x6, 0xC] and we want the row mask for output bit o = 2.

Step 1 — for each data bit j, check if column j has bit 2 set:

j	B_cols[j]	bit 2 set?
0	0x7 = 0111	yes
1	0xE = 1110	yes
2	0x5 = 0101	yes
3	0xA = 1010	no
4	0xD = 1101	yes
5	0x3 = 0011	no
6	0x6 = 0110	yes
7	0xC = 1100	yes
Step 2 — assemble the mask:

text
mask[2] = bit 0 | bit 1 | bit 2 | bit 4 | bit 6 | bit 7
        = 0b11100111
        = 0xE7
Step 3 — assemble all row masks this way and concatenate them into a packed constant. For the toy:

text
B_MASK_rows[0] = 0xE3   (bits 0,1,5,6,7)
B_MASK_rows[1] = 0xF6   (bits 1,2,4,5,6,7)
B_MASK_rows[2] = 0xE7   (bits 0,1,2,4,6,7)
B_MASK_rows[3] = 0x5A   (bits 1,3,4,6)
The Python generator loops exactly this and emits a hex literal per row.

G. Group splitting — with numbers
G.1 The identity
text
B · data = XOR_{g=0}^{D-1} (B_g · data_g)
where data_g is the slice of data for group g (contiguous bits), and B_g is the submatrix of B restricted to the columns of group g.

G.2 Toy partition
Take W = 8 bits, D = 2 groups of 4 bits. Then:

text
group 0 covers data bits [0, 4)
group 1 covers data bits [4, 8)
Split B_cols:

text
B_0_cols = B_cols[0:4] = [0x7, 0xE, 0x5, 0xA]
B_1_cols = B_cols[4:8] = [0xD, 0x3, 0x6, 0xC]
G.3 Numerical check
data = 0b10110101 → group 0 = 0b0101, group 1 = 0b1011.

Group 0 contribution (bits 0 and 2 set):

text
B_0[0] XOR B_0[2] = 0x7 XOR 0x5 = 0x2
Group 1 contribution (bits 4, 5, 7 set within the group → global bits 0, 1, 3):

text
B_1[0] XOR B_1[1] XOR B_1[3] = 0xD XOR 0x3 XOR 0xC = 0x2
Group sum: 0x2 XOR 0x2 = 0x0.

Full B · data (bits 0, 2, 4, 5, 7 set):

text
B_cols[0] XOR B_cols[2] XOR B_cols[4] XOR B_cols[5] XOR B_cols[7]
= 0x7 XOR 0x5 XOR 0xD XOR 0x3 XOR 0xC
= 0x0  ✓
Group split matches full-width. This is the identity Section 3 of the original dry-run verifies once per D value.

H. The B_GROUP1 mask — where the extra 256 bits come from
B_GROUP0_MASK_PACKED covers data bits [0, 256). B_GROUP1_MASK_PACKED covers data bits [256, 512) — but the RTL slices st_pipe_bus[i].tdata[256 +: 256], i.e., the global bits [256, 512) of the beat.

The generator's group_masks(g) function produces, for group g:

python
lo, hi = (g*256, (g+1)*256)
for j in range(lo, hi):
    local_bit = j - lo                       # position WITHIN the group
    for k in range(32):
        if (B_cols[j] >> k) & 1:
            masks[k] |= (1 << local_bit)
Two things to note:

The generator consumes global B_cols[j] — this encodes the propagation of global bit j all the way to the end of the 1024-bit beat, not just to the end of the group.

The mask position is local — the mask only spans GW = 256 bits, and bit local_bit of the mask corresponds to global bit j = lo + local_bit.

That is why the group masks are GW-wide but their columns are drawn from the full-width B_cols. The group is a domain restriction, not a time truncation.

H.1 Toy verification
With W = 8, D = 2, group 1 uses lo, hi = (4, 8):

text
B_1_cols = [B_cols[4], B_cols[5], B_cols[6], B_cols[7]]
         = [0xD, 0x3, 0x6, 0xC]
Now suppose we want output bit o = 0 of B_GROUP1_MASK_PACKED:

j = 4: B_cols[4] = 0xD = 1101, bit 0 set → local_bit 0 → mask bit 0 set

j = 5: B_cols[5] = 0x3 = 0011, bit 0 set → local_bit 1 → mask bit 1 set

j = 6: B_cols[6] = 0x6 = 0110, bit 0 clear → no

j = 7: B_cols[7] = 0xC = 1100, bit 0 clear → no

B_GROUP1_MASK_rows[0] = 0b0011 = 0x3.

Check by applying the mask to group 1 slice 0b1011 = 0xB:

text
^((0xB) & 0x3) = ^(0b1011 & 0b0011) = ^(0b0011) = 1 XOR 1 = 0
And from column form:

text
B_1[0] XOR B_1[2] XOR B_1[3]     (since 0xB = 1011 has bits 0, 1, 3)
Hmm, let me redo. 0xB = 1011 in MSB-first writing, but we're using LSB-first. 0xB = 0b1011 means bit 0 = 1, bit 1 = 1, bit 2 = 0, bit 3 = 1.

Column form: contribution from bit 0 (global bit 4), bit 1 (global bit 5), bit 3 (global bit 7):

text
B_1[0] XOR B_1[1] XOR B_1[3] = 0xD XOR 0x3 XOR 0xC = 0x2
Bit 0 of 0x2 = 0. ✓

Mask form gave 0 for output bit 0. Match.

